s($,"b0b","lW",()=>{var q=2026
return A.b([A.fg("I have completed the notes on the Analytical Engine. Note G contains the algorithm for computing Bernoulli numbers, worked through in full so that the machine's operations can be followed step by step. The engine might act upon things besides number, were objects found whose mutual relations could be expressed by the abstract science of operations. It could compose elaborate pieces of music of any degree of complexity.",1,"I have completed the notes on the Analytical Engine.","Ada Lovelace",A.ff(q,8,24,9,12),"Analytical Engine notes"),A.fg("The Mark II stopped again this morning and it took us most of an hour to find the cause. It was a moth, trapped in relay 70 of panel F. I have taped it into the logbook with the time it was found, so from now on, when the machine misbehaves, we are quite literally debugging it.",2,"The Mark II stopped again this morning and it took us most of an hour to find the cause.","Grace Hopper",A.ff(q,8,24,8,3),"Found a bug (literally)"),A.fg("The paper is ready for your comments. The machine I describe need not be complicated: a tape, a head, and a table of behaviour are enough to compute anything that can be computed at all. What matters is the negative result \u2014 some numbers cannot be computed by any such machine, and no cleverness of engineering will change that.",3,"The paper is ready for your comments.","Alan Turing",A.ff(q,8,23,17,41),"On computable numbers"),A.fg("He would not fly until I had checked the figures myself, so I ran the whole re-entry by hand. They agree with the machine to the fifth decimal place. Tell him the capsule will come down where we said it would, and that the arithmetic does not care who does it.",4,"He would not fly until I had checked the figures myself, so I ran the whole re-entry by hand.","Katherine Johnson",A.ff(q,8,23,14,20),"Trajectory verification"),A.fg("The 1202 alarms during descent were the computer telling us it was overloaded with rendezvous radar data it did not need. Because the software knew which tasks mattered, it shed the rest and kept the landing programs running. The alarm was not a failure; it was the design working exactly as intended.",5,"The 1202 alarms during descent were the computer telling us it was overloaded with rendezvous radar data it did not need.","Margaret Hamilton",A.ff(q,8,23,11,7),"Priority displays saved the landing"),A.fg("The language is small enough to fit in one head and close enough to the machine to stay fast, which is the whole trick. We have rewritten the kernel in it, which we are told is either brave or foolish. It compiles, it boots, and it is a great deal easier to move to another machine than assembler ever was.",6,"The language is small enough to fit in one head and close enough to the machine to stay fast, which is the whole trick.","Dennis Ritchie",A.ff(q,8,22,16,55),"C and Unix update"),A.fg("Here is the draft. What is wanted is a substitution property: if S is a subtype of T, then objects of type T may be replaced with objects of type S without altering any of the desirable properties of the program. Inheritance that violates this is not abstraction \u2014 it is a trap laid for whoever maintains the code next.",7,"Here is the draft.","Barbara Liskov",A.ff(q,8,22,10,38),"Substitution principle draft"),A.fg("The proposal is for a linked information system, so that a reader can move from one document to another without knowing or caring where either one lives. Universality is the entire point: a web that only works inside one building, or one vendor's software, is not a web at all.",8,"The proposal is for a linked information system, so that a reader can move from one document to another without knowing or caring where either one lives.","Tim Berners-Lee",A.ff(q,8,21,15,12),"A proposal: the Web"),A.fg("Volume 4 has grown into several fascicles, because combinatorial algorithms refuse to be brief and I refuse to hand-wave. I would rather be late and right than prompt and approximate. In the meantime the exercises are numbered by difficulty, and I have paid out rather more reward cheques than I expected.",9,"Volume 4 has grown into several fascicles, because combinatorial algorithms refuse to be brief and I refuse to hand-wave.","Donald Knuth",A.ff(q,8,21,9,44),"Volume 4 is taking a while"),A.fg("My thesis is that the go to statement should be abolished from all higher level programming languages. The quality of programmers is a decreasing function of the density of go to statements in the programs they produce. Our powers to visualise processes evolving in time are poorly developed; we should not squander them.",10,"My thesis is that the go to statement should be abolished from all higher level programming languages.","Edsger Dijkstra",A.ff(q,8,20,13,29),"A case against the goto"),A.fg("The fundamental problem of communication is that of reproducing at one point, either exactly or approximately, a message selected at another point. The semantic aspects are irrelevant to the engineering problem. Once you measure information in bits, noise stops being a nuisance and becomes something you can budget for.",11,"The fundamental problem of communication is that of reproducing at one point, either exactly or approximately, a message selected at another point.","Claude Shannon",A.ff(q,8,20,8,15),"A mathematical theory of communication"),A.fg("If the instructions live in the same memory as the data they act upon, then a machine can read and even modify its own programme. One store, one addressing scheme, and the machine stops being a fixed calculator and becomes something we can point at any problem we can describe.",12,"If the instructions live in the same memory as the data they act upon, then a machine can read and even modify its own programme.","John von Neumann",A.ff(q,8,19,18,2),"Storing the program as data"),A.fg("If the transmitter and the receiver change frequency together on a shared schedule, a jammer cannot follow them and a listener hears only fragments. The piano roll suggested the mechanism to us: eighty-eight frequencies, changed in step. The patent is filed; whether the Navy reads it is another matter.",13,"If the transmitter and the receiver change frequency together on a shared schedule, a jammer cannot follow them and a listener hears only fragments.","Hedy Lamarr",A.ff(q,8,19,12,47),"Frequency hopping for secure signals"),A.fg("The trick is not to make the networks the same, but to let them stay different. A common protocol at the gateway is enough, provided every network agrees on how a packet is addressed and who is responsible when one goes missing. Keep the middle simple and let the edges be clever.",14,"The trick is not to make the networks the same, but to let them stay different.","Vint Cerf",A.ff(q,8,18,16,30),"A protocol for interconnecting networks"),A.fg("Bridges that forward everything will happily forward it forever, and one loop takes down the whole network. Compute a spanning tree, disable the redundant links, and keep them ready for the moment the topology changes. The algorithm fits on the back of an envelope, which is rather the point.",15,"Bridges that forward everything will happily forward it forever, and one loop takes down the whole network.","Radia Perlman",A.ff(q,8,18,10,11),"Spanning tree keeps the bridges sane"),A.fg("I'm doing a free operating system \u2014 just a hobby, won't be big and professional like GNU \u2014 for 386 AT clones. It has been brewing since April and has finally reached the stage where it is even usable. I'd like any feedback on things people like or dislike, and on what features you would want.",16,"I'm doing a free operating system \u2014 just a hobby, won't be big and professional like GNU \u2014 for 386 AT clones.","Linus Torvalds",A.ff(q,8,17,20,58),"Doing a (free) operating system")],A.am("z<md>"))})
